"Screens concepts by comparing each criterion with a shared reference concept using better, similar or worse judgments.\n\nUse when: Compare early concepts before reliable absolute scores exist. Identify combinations of strengths that could improve a reference design.\n\nAvoid when: Precise validated measurements and an absolute weighted utility model are available.\n\nVisual grammar: One reference concept is the comparison datum. Criteria rows intersect concept columns. Plus, same and minus marks encode relative desirability, with any weighting explicit.\n\nBuild: Choose a credible baseline and define discriminating criteria. Compare each concept with that same datum criterion by criterion. Discuss weak points and synthesize improved concepts before another review.\n\nCheck: All ratings compare against the same named baseline. Positive marks consistently mean more desirable performance. Unknown evidence is distinguished from an equal-to-baseline judgment.\n\nPitfalls: Treating plus and minus counts as proof of optimality. Changing the reference silently between concepts.\n\nExample: A packaging team compares recyclable carton concepts with its current box, then combines the strongest closure and stacking features.\n\nAlternatives: Weighted decision matrix, Morphological chart."
